{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789321361,"nonce":"Dqzg7lgMCBri0+8uQ0l4c0jlPSs=","title":"A string made by `+` is never released","message":"s = \"\"\n    for i in range(40000):\n        s = s + \"x\"\n\npeaks at 971 MB: the sum of every intermediate length, so no intermediate is ever freed. ZynML shows the same on the same loop (926 MB). This is the memory half of the join crash (c1e4670); with join linear it remains for every program that accumulates a string.\n\n`$IO$string_concat` returns fresh storage the caller must free with `$IO$string_free`, but `drop_insert::symbol_role` classes every `$IO$`/`$String$` symbol as a borrower of its arguments and says nothing about their results, so a concat result is never an allocation site. `release_loop_carried` would also refuse the accumulator: its entry incoming is the literal `\"\"`, which is not storage the function owns, and it only handles single-block loop bodies.\n\nWhat it takes:\n- A string-returning `$IO$`/`$String$` symbol call is an allocation site released by `$IO$string_free` (the call's result type says which calls return strings).\n- A loop-carried string phi seeded by something the function does not own gets a `string_copy` on the entry edge, so every incoming is owned and the accumulator can be released after its last use in the body; the back-edge value's own uses must all be borrows too (today's guard checks only the phi's), or a value pushed into a list would be freed by the next iteration.\n- Multi-block loop bodies: place the release with `drop_points`-style liveness from the header rather than requiring one body block.\n\nAlso seen while here: `release_loop_carried` inserts `Release::Intrinsic` regardless of the site's release kind, so a loop-carried box would be freed with `free` rather than `zyntax_box_free`.","files":null}]}